WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:04.800
So we are heading into San Francisco.

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:10.100
I'm going to be interviewing
the co-CEO of Waymo today.

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:11.640
If you live in San Francisco,

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.760
you've probably seen Waymos everywhere.

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:19.220
Google self-driving robotaxis
are taking over their home

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:22.640
turf and slowly taking
their show on the road

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.320
across the US and around the world.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:27.540
So we're going to play a little game.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:28.960
It's called Count the Waymos.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.940
Every time we see a Waymo,
we're going to count it.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:38.700
There's one. One. Another one? Two. Five.

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:41.500
Wonder what this one is doing.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:43.260
Oh no, it's trying to get around this car.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:46.580
It's being very patient. Good Waymo. Good.

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:49.520
Seven, eight, two in a row. Oh my gosh.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.720
Okay, so that was nine, 10, 11, 12,

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:55.840
14, 15, two more.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:57.520
Is that another one? 16.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:00.720
Okay, so we've been driving, I
don't know, about 15 minutes.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:05.200
We've seen 16 Waymos, so that's
more than a Waymo a minute,

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:07.420
which is a lot of Waymos.

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:14.240
But as Waymo's fleet has grown,

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.660
so has scrutiny over its safety record.

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:18.460
- Hi, we are currently blocking a train.

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:20.920
Driving right past the active police stop.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:24.760
Heavy rainfall, landing Waymo's
robo taxis in hot water.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:27.560
That leaves Waymo's
co-CEO Tekedra Mawakana

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:29.900
with a difficult job,
convincing the public

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:32.980
to trust a technology
that still makes mistakes.

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:36.300
I think it's important
for society to grapple with

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:41.000
that question of how uncomfortable
am I with the status quo?

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.820
Is it worth it to get an improvement

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:47.420
by embracing this new
technology that isn't promising

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:49.841
to be perfect, but at the same time,

00:01:49.841 --> 00:01:52.381
it can really improve road safety.

00:01:57.821 --> 00:02:02.761
Hello.
Hi. Let's go for a ride.

00:02:02.761 --> 00:02:07.761
All right, here we go. I
remember my first ride, 2011.

00:02:08.041 --> 00:02:09.961
Oh, wow.
I was with Sebastian Thrun.

00:02:09.961 --> 00:02:13.261
Yes.
Who founded the Google car team.

00:02:13.261 --> 00:02:16.141
Yes. Are you ready?
I think so.

00:02:16.141 --> 00:02:19.781
Now we are on a freeway
in self-driving mode.

00:02:19.781 --> 00:02:21.861
This is incredible. I mean,

00:02:21.861 --> 00:02:26.301
even then it was impressive. It was jerky.

00:02:26.301 --> 00:02:28.021
Yeah.
I was definitely a little nervous.

00:02:28.021 --> 00:02:29.701
Do you remember your first ride?

00:02:29.701 --> 00:02:33.341
I do. It was very, very early days

00:02:33.341 --> 00:02:35.001
and I though, you know what?

00:02:35.001 --> 00:02:37.621
This becomes normal so fast.

00:02:37.621 --> 00:02:38.801
Even though I'm a tech journalist

00:02:38.801 --> 00:02:42.441
and very tech forward, I'm
still one of those parents

00:02:42.441 --> 00:02:44.541
that doesn't want to put
my kid in a way mode.

00:02:44.541 --> 00:02:45.801
What do you say to the people

00:02:45.801 --> 00:02:47.301
out there who still think like me?

00:02:47.301 --> 00:02:51.021
My experience has been
that people normally get

00:02:51.021 --> 00:02:54.101
to the other side of
that by enjoying using

00:02:54.101 --> 00:02:55.581
and experiencing the technology

00:02:55.581 --> 00:02:57.661
firsthand more and more and more.

00:02:57.661 --> 00:02:59.541
I haven't though about
it for a second actually.

00:02:59.541 --> 00:03:00.701
I know.
Been sitting here.

00:03:00.701 --> 00:03:03.701
So often people get in
and they are whispering

00:03:03.701 --> 00:03:05.121
and they're like, "So anyway, tell me

00:03:05.121 --> 00:03:07.341
what happened." And then they're like,

00:03:07.341 --> 00:03:08.561
"Why am I whispering?"

00:03:10.781 --> 00:03:13.881
Mawakana got her start as
a lawyer in the mid '90s,

00:03:13.881 --> 00:03:17.741
then worked at Internet 1.0
flagships like AOL, Yahoo,

00:03:17.741 --> 00:03:20.061
and eBay before joining Waymo.

00:03:20.061 --> 00:03:23.861
She became the company's co-
CEO along with Dmitri Dolgov,

00:03:23.861 --> 00:03:26.541
one of the founders of
Google's original self-driving

00:03:26.541 --> 00:03:30.081
project, a group tracing back
to a Stanford engineering team

00:03:30.081 --> 00:03:33.021
that won DARPA's 2005 Grand Challenge,

00:03:33.021 --> 00:03:36.462
sending a driverless car
132 miles across the desert.

00:03:37.461 --> 00:03:39.782
Very few companies that are this big,

00:03:39.782 --> 00:03:41.302
this high profile have co-CEOs.

00:03:41.302 --> 00:03:43.402
Yeah. How is that working for you?

00:03:43.402 --> 00:03:44.822
It's working really well.

00:03:44.822 --> 00:03:49.822
I always say that this specific
business really requires

00:03:50.002 --> 00:03:51.942
world-class engineering talent

00:03:51.942 --> 00:03:54.062
and Dmitri leads a team

00:03:54.062 --> 00:03:56.842
of world-class engineers on
the hardware and the software.

00:03:56.842 --> 00:03:59.622
And it also requires a really deliberate

00:03:59.622 --> 00:04:01.562
and thoughtful approach to going to market

00:04:01.562 --> 00:04:02.862
and commercializing.

00:04:02.862 --> 00:04:04.802
And then at the same time, a lot

00:04:04.802 --> 00:04:06.742
of leaders are quite lonely.

00:04:06.742 --> 00:04:07.962
It's lonely at the top. And

00:04:07.962 --> 00:04:10.322
so I think it's also been
fun to have a copilot.

00:04:10.322 --> 00:04:11.502
You were born in Mississippi.

00:04:11.502 --> 00:04:12.922
Your dad was in the Air Force.

00:04:12.922 --> 00:04:16.042
You moved around a lot as a
kid. How did that shape you?

00:04:16.042 --> 00:04:18.422
My dad's family owned a farm.

00:04:18.422 --> 00:04:20.162
Farming is hard work,

00:04:20.162 --> 00:04:22.902
and at the same time, it's very rewarding.

00:04:22.902 --> 00:04:25.182
You actually see things grow.

00:04:25.182 --> 00:04:28.382
You get to pick them,
consume them, sell them.

00:04:28.382 --> 00:04:31.922
And so there was something
about understanding a

00:04:31.922 --> 00:04:34.162
self-sustaining system,

00:04:34.162 --> 00:04:37.962
and I really learned that
from my family in Mississippi.

00:04:37.962 --> 00:04:41.062
Were you into computers when
you were little or cars? No.

00:04:41.062 --> 00:04:43.322
Was running a self-driving
car company ever

00:04:43.322 --> 00:04:44.882
on your bingo card?

00:04:44.882 --> 00:04:49.762
No. No. I mean, I, as I got
older, have been into cars.

00:04:49.762 --> 00:04:51.522
I like to drive.

00:04:51.522 --> 00:04:53.462
Sometimes people think
I don't like to drive

00:04:53.462 --> 00:04:55.402
because they're like, "You're
trying to eliminate driving?"

00:04:55.402 --> 00:04:56.822
I'm like, "No, I'm trying

00:04:56.822 --> 00:04:59.102
to eliminate driving when it's not fun."

00:04:59.102 --> 00:05:01.142
But I love a top down,

00:05:01.142 --> 00:05:04.022
good music blasting on
my way to the beach.

00:05:04.022 --> 00:05:05.842
So no, it was not on my bingo card.

00:05:11.562 --> 00:05:13.362
Despite their growing visibility,

00:05:13.362 --> 00:05:16.662
a 2026 Pew Research poll found 71%

00:05:16.662 --> 00:05:17.902
of adults say they would feel

00:05:17.902 --> 00:05:19.702
uncomfortable in a driverless car.

00:05:20.622 --> 00:05:23.642
What's the first word that
comes to mind when I say Waymo?

00:05:23.642 --> 00:05:25.863
Empty. Yeah. A little scary.

00:05:25.863 --> 00:05:27.463
The though of a driverless car doesn't

00:05:27.463 --> 00:05:30.443
really make me feel safe.

00:05:30.443 --> 00:05:32.983
I've been in one maybe 20 times or so.

00:05:32.983 --> 00:05:34.983
I just like to embrace new technologies.

00:05:34.983 --> 00:05:36.023
I've had enough experience with it now

00:05:36.023 --> 00:05:37.783
that it does feel safe enough.

00:05:38.903 --> 00:05:41.123
According to a study from
the Insurance Institute

00:05:41.123 --> 00:05:43.823
for Highway Safety, an
independent nonprofit,

00:05:43.823 --> 00:05:47.603
Waymos in four cities were
involved in 68% fewer crashes

00:05:47.603 --> 00:05:50.923
than human drivers and
had a 91% lower rate

00:05:50.923 --> 00:05:52.323
of rear-ending another vehicle.

00:05:53.223 --> 00:05:54.923
But in the age of smartphones,

00:05:54.923 --> 00:05:57.823
every wayward Waymo has
a shot at going viral.

00:05:57.823 --> 00:05:59.523
The vehicles become confused

00:05:59.523 --> 00:06:01.283
and start honking all at each other.

00:06:02.363 --> 00:06:07.043
This passenger in Georgia
trapped inside a Waymo. Oh, hi.

00:06:07.043 --> 00:06:08.043
She can't get out the
car.

00:06:08.043 --> 00:06:09.263
I didn't know you were in there.

00:06:09.263 --> 00:06:11.023
I feel like every day there's a news story

00:06:11.023 --> 00:06:12.583
about what happened in a Waymo.

00:06:12.583 --> 00:06:14.783
Waymos have been driving
through school bus signs,

00:06:14.783 --> 00:06:17.463
construction zones, flood zones.

00:06:17.463 --> 00:06:20.423
Many of those are
operational challenges, right?

00:06:20.423 --> 00:06:23.443
Like what do you do when the
entire network goes down?

00:06:23.443 --> 00:06:27.943
How robust are our
internal operational plans?

00:06:27.943 --> 00:06:30.943
And then how closely aligned
are we with city officials?

00:06:30.943 --> 00:06:32.523
And so we're building out that

00:06:32.523 --> 00:06:34.403
capability and we're learning.

00:06:34.403 --> 00:06:36.983
It's like we don't show up
and say we know everything,

00:06:36.983 --> 00:06:39.483
and at the same time,
we're always improving.

00:06:39.483 --> 00:06:41.183
So we use simulation.

00:06:41.183 --> 00:06:44.583
We ingest data about things
we've never encountered ever.

00:06:46.123 --> 00:06:48.623
And then we come up with
a thousand scenarios

00:06:48.623 --> 00:06:52.203
that are adjacent to the
thing that we are ingesting,

00:06:52.203 --> 00:06:54.603
and then things happen that
are still a combination

00:06:54.603 --> 00:06:57.103
of factors that haven't
yet been simulated.

00:06:58.163 --> 00:07:01.243
GM's former self-driving
unit cruise showed just

00:07:01.243 --> 00:07:03.283
how quickly things can unravel.

00:07:03.283 --> 00:07:07.123
In 2023, another car knocked
a pedestrian into the path

00:07:07.123 --> 00:07:08.643
of a self-driving cruise,

00:07:08.643 --> 00:07:10.943
which then dragged her about 20 feet.

00:07:10.943 --> 00:07:12.903
California suspended cruise,

00:07:12.903 --> 00:07:15.064
and a year later, GM pulled the plug.

00:07:16.244 --> 00:07:20.284
What happens if Waymo has
a catastrophic accident?

00:07:20.284 --> 00:07:22.704
I think it's important
for us to be super humble

00:07:22.704 --> 00:07:27.264
and assume because even as
we work to eliminate all

00:07:27.264 --> 00:07:30.504
of these crashes, we know
the number won't be zero.

00:07:30.504 --> 00:07:32.864
And so knowing that
means that we also know

00:07:32.864 --> 00:07:34.824
that there will be an incident,

00:07:34.824 --> 00:07:38.704
and I think it'll be a really
challenging day for us.

00:07:38.704 --> 00:07:40.384
You were asked at a conference,

00:07:40.384 --> 00:07:42.244
"Will society accept a death

00:07:42.244 --> 00:07:44.244
by a robot?" And you said something like,

00:07:44.244 --> 00:07:45.724
"I think society will." And

00:07:45.724 --> 00:07:47.784
that comment got a lot of pushback.

00:07:47.784 --> 00:07:49.984
How do you reflect on that now?

00:07:49.984 --> 00:07:53.984
I think I would
rephrase the answer to say

00:07:54.864 --> 00:07:58.844
society is already
accepting a lot of deaths

00:08:00.104 --> 00:08:01.964
caused by human drivers.

00:08:01.964 --> 00:08:05.284
I think it's important for
society to grapple with

00:08:05.284 --> 00:08:09.964
that question of how uncomfortable
am I with the status quo?

00:08:09.964 --> 00:08:13.764
Is it worth it to get an improvement

00:08:13.764 --> 00:08:17.744
by embracing this new
technology that isn't promising

00:08:17.744 --> 00:08:20.144
to be perfect, but at the same time,

00:08:20.144 --> 00:08:22.684
it can really improve road safety?

00:08:23.724 --> 00:08:26.644
When a Waymo runs into a
problem like this car blocking

00:08:26.644 --> 00:08:28.844
emergency personnel at a deadly fire,

00:08:28.844 --> 00:08:30.804
remote operators can provide guidance.

00:08:30.804 --> 00:08:31.884
Hello, can you hear me?

00:08:31.884 --> 00:08:33.304
Yes, you need to move this car ASAP.

00:08:33.304 --> 00:08:36.784
The system seems to be
having a minor issue.

00:08:36.784 --> 00:08:38.004
But Waymo says these people

00:08:38.004 --> 00:08:41.184
behind the scenes don't
actually drive the vehicles.

00:08:41.184 --> 00:08:43.604
This became a flashpoint at
a Senate hearing when Waymo's

00:08:43.604 --> 00:08:45.584
chief safety officer disclosed that some

00:08:45.584 --> 00:08:48.224
of the operators are
based in the Philippines.

00:08:48.224 --> 00:08:52.864
Having people overseas
influencing American vehicles is a

00:08:52.864 --> 00:08:54.524
safety issue.

00:08:54.524 --> 00:08:56.224
That really freaked people out.

00:08:56.224 --> 00:08:58.924
I think people were like,
"Ooh, I didn't realize that."

00:08:58.924 --> 00:09:01.085
There's not a good
understanding of the distinction

00:09:01.085 --> 00:09:04.625
between fully autonomous
and partial autonomy.

00:09:04.625 --> 00:09:07.505
And so when those questions
are asked about remote workers

00:09:07.505 --> 00:09:10.225
and driving the vehicle,
that's immediately

00:09:10.225 --> 00:09:13.065
what it sounds like, is like,
"Oh my gosh, I didn't know

00:09:13.065 --> 00:09:16.425
that Waymo hires people to
drive their cars offsite,"

00:09:16.425 --> 00:09:17.745
and that's not what we do.

00:09:18.645 --> 00:09:21.125
And so I understand the confusion,

00:09:21.125 --> 00:09:24.265
but when you're being interrogated,

00:09:24.265 --> 00:09:25.565
it sounds like one and the same.

00:09:25.565 --> 00:09:27.585
Is the steering wheel
ever going to go away?

00:09:27.585 --> 00:09:30.125
Yeah. I mean, I think
over time, absolutely.

00:09:30.125 --> 00:09:32.025
And what about the pedals
and the driver's seat?

00:09:32.025 --> 00:09:34.205
I think a lot of that could follow.

00:09:35.485 --> 00:09:37.545
We haven't rushed to do that

00:09:37.545 --> 00:09:41.505
because we recognize that
there are still these moments

00:09:41.505 --> 00:09:44.465
where first responders,
tow truck companies need

00:09:44.465 --> 00:09:46.805
to move the vehicle, and
we want them to have access

00:09:46.805 --> 00:09:48.645
to doing so as quickly as possible.

00:09:48.645 --> 00:09:53.225
But safety isn't the only
thing people think about

00:09:53.225 --> 00:09:54.325
before jumping into Waymo.

00:09:55.545 --> 00:09:57.805
When I have rode like in Ubers

00:09:57.805 --> 00:10:00.785
or Lyft, it does feel uncomfortable
being with a stranger.

00:10:00.785 --> 00:10:02.745
If Waymo was to be a little cheaper,

00:10:02.745 --> 00:10:04.945
then I would rather do Waymo.

00:10:04.945 --> 00:10:06.845
Personally, I've never
used it for many reasons,

00:10:06.845 --> 00:10:08.805
but one of the main reasons is price.

00:10:08.805 --> 00:10:10.325
According to the latest research,

00:10:10.325 --> 00:10:14.925
Waymo rides average $9.58
per mile, compared with 688

00:10:14.925 --> 00:10:17.265
for Uber and 641 for Lyft.

00:10:18.285 --> 00:10:19.765
My son just said this morning, "Wait,

00:10:19.765 --> 00:10:22.225
if it's more expensive, why
wouldn't you just take an Uber?"

00:10:22.225 --> 00:10:23.545
How do you get the cost down?

00:10:23.545 --> 00:10:26.625
What we think is
important is that it is a

00:10:26.625 --> 00:10:28.545
very consistent experience.

00:10:28.545 --> 00:10:32.385
And so if everyone's commuting
at the exact same time, yes,

00:10:32.385 --> 00:10:35.145
the prices go up because
we have a fixed fleet.

00:10:35.145 --> 00:10:37.805
So would you say price
is not the first priority?

00:10:37.805 --> 00:10:40.185
Price is not the first priority.

00:10:41.185 --> 00:10:43.745
It is important though that we match it

00:10:43.745 --> 00:10:46.165
with the experience that we're offering.

00:10:48.565 --> 00:10:51.266
At the center of that
experience is Waymo's self-driving

00:10:51.266 --> 00:10:53.886
technology integrated into vehicles made

00:10:53.886 --> 00:10:55.886
by established car makers,

00:10:55.886 --> 00:10:58.566
and that puts Waymo in
an unusual position.

00:10:58.566 --> 00:11:00.006
It's both a tech company

00:11:00.006 --> 00:11:01.966
and a transportation service

00:11:01.966 --> 00:11:03.766
with competitors on multiple fronts.

00:11:05.326 --> 00:11:07.146
Who is Waymo really competing with?

00:11:07.146 --> 00:11:10.626
Is it ride-hailing companies
like Uber? Is it Tesla?

00:11:10.626 --> 00:11:12.826
Is it startups like Wave? Is it Zoox?

00:11:12.826 --> 00:11:14.806
Or is it just the car in my driveway?

00:11:14.806 --> 00:11:19.406
I think we are competing
with any car that is unsafe,

00:11:19.406 --> 00:11:22.726
and we've started with
ride-hailing as our primary focus

00:11:22.726 --> 00:11:25.086
for this business, but we've always said

00:11:25.086 --> 00:11:27.646
that we will have other lines of business,

00:11:27.646 --> 00:11:31.806
including licensing our
technology to automotive companies

00:11:31.806 --> 00:11:33.446
to make it available for sale.

00:11:33.446 --> 00:11:36.386
But eventually, you'll
have this question of like,

00:11:36.386 --> 00:11:39.386
do I want the car in my
driveway sitting there?

00:11:39.386 --> 00:11:40.486
Do I want to own it?

00:11:40.486 --> 00:11:44.206
Do I want it to stay on the
move when I'm in the office?

00:11:44.206 --> 00:11:45.866
What is the long-term goal?

00:11:45.866 --> 00:11:48.886
The long-term goal is for every vehicle

00:11:48.886 --> 00:11:52.766
to perform in a superhuman safe manner so

00:11:52.766 --> 00:11:57.646
that we can eliminate some very
large percentage of the 1.2

00:11:57.646 --> 00:12:01.306
million road deaths that
are happening globally.

00:12:01.306 --> 00:12:03.786
I mean, we know there's a
very large percentage of those

00:12:03.786 --> 00:12:05.386
that can be prevented,

00:12:05.386 --> 00:12:06.986
and we think there's a kind

00:12:06.986 --> 00:12:09.886
of a responsibility to try to do so.

00:12:14.646 --> 00:12:16.326
But before they can hit the road,

00:12:16.326 --> 00:12:18.426
or maybe one day, your driveway...

00:12:18.426 --> 00:12:20.206
Bye, Waymo. Drive safe.

00:12:21.126 --> 00:12:23.226
Waymo's fleet goes through a glow-up at a

00:12:23.226 --> 00:12:24.726
facility outside of Phoenix.

00:12:25.946 --> 00:12:27.606
That's where I met Amanda Bell,

00:12:27.606 --> 00:12:29.486
who leads Waymo's manufacturing site.

00:12:30.726 --> 00:12:33.626
So what happens right now,
the vehicle just rolled on.

00:12:33.626 --> 00:12:35.286
You can see kind of some of the cutouts

00:12:35.286 --> 00:12:38.107
and the content that has
already been removed for us,

00:12:38.107 --> 00:12:40.527
and there's just some small
disassembly that we do,

00:12:40.527 --> 00:12:42.187
and we start the production.

00:12:44.387 --> 00:12:47.347
The start and finish, we
can do it in one shift.

00:12:47.347 --> 00:12:49.747
So you can get a car
fully outfitted in one day.

00:12:49.747 --> 00:12:53.187
Yes, absolutely.
Google had a plan

00:12:53.187 --> 00:12:55.967
to build its own car, scrapped that plan.

00:12:55.967 --> 00:12:58.467
Would Waymo ever build its own car again?

00:12:58.467 --> 00:13:00.987
I think that for us,
our position is really we

00:13:00.987 --> 00:13:02.307
want to do strengths on strengths.

00:13:02.307 --> 00:13:04.527
We're a tech company. We have all

00:13:04.527 --> 00:13:06.127
of this incredible knowledge

00:13:06.127 --> 00:13:09.507
and know-how to be able to
build an autonomous vehicle.

00:13:09.507 --> 00:13:12.067
But then on the vehicle side, there's OEMs

00:13:12.067 --> 00:13:14.807
that have been doing this
for hundreds of years

00:13:14.807 --> 00:13:16.607
and they know the vehicle.

00:13:16.607 --> 00:13:18.267
Jaguar, the OEM

00:13:18.267 --> 00:13:19.100
or original equipment manufacturer

00:13:19.100 --> 00:13:19.933
behind Waymo's I-PACE

00:13:19.933 --> 00:13:22.766
fleet has discontinued that model.

00:13:23.967 --> 00:13:27.467
Now, Waymo is scaling its
electric minivan, the Ojai,

00:13:27.467 --> 00:13:29.267
which has attracted some controversy.

00:13:30.127 --> 00:13:32.067
Your Ojai fleet is built by Zeeker

00:13:32.067 --> 00:13:36.867
which is a Chinese car maker,
then outfitted by Waymo.

00:13:36.867 --> 00:13:38.767
That's right.
One senator has accused Waymo

00:13:38.767 --> 00:13:40.387
of getting in bed with China.

00:13:40.387 --> 00:13:41.627
How do you respond to that?

00:13:41.627 --> 00:13:44.987
We receive the vehicles
here in the United States

00:13:44.987 --> 00:13:47.527
and we have fixed the
Waymo driver, the software,

00:13:47.527 --> 00:13:50.147
the hardware, our full sensor suite

00:13:50.147 --> 00:13:52.587
in our facility in Phoenix, Arizona.

00:13:52.587 --> 00:13:55.667
I mean, this is the intellectual property

00:13:55.667 --> 00:13:58.747
and mind share of a US-based team.

00:13:58.747 --> 00:14:00.187
Why not use American cars?

00:14:00.187 --> 00:14:02.627
Yeah, we think it is
important to continue

00:14:02.627 --> 00:14:05.267
to explore partnerships
with US auto companies.

00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:10.507
I think that a lot of
people maybe think, oh,

00:14:10.507 --> 00:14:13.307
you could just take a car
and slap on the components.

00:14:13.307 --> 00:14:14.847
It's not that by any means.

00:14:14.847 --> 00:14:17.227
It's a couple years long
process, so we also want them

00:14:17.227 --> 00:14:20.387
to be thinking and considering
their design of their vehicle

00:14:20.387 --> 00:14:21.567
and stepping outside of the box.

00:14:25.647 --> 00:14:26.768
What's going here?
So

00:14:26.768 --> 00:14:28.108
that's going to be one of our pods.

00:14:28.108 --> 00:14:31.408
And so one of the pods on the
front fender, the pod is going

00:14:31.408 --> 00:14:34.128
to actually have cameras and sensors,

00:14:34.128 --> 00:14:36.888
and then it will also have the
LiDAR spinning in the front,

00:14:36.888 --> 00:14:38.428
and so it can see up

00:14:38.428 --> 00:14:42.188
to three football fields
in a 360 degree radius

00:14:42.188 --> 00:14:43.888
around the vehicle at all times.

00:14:45.848 --> 00:14:49.308
Waymo's tech stack uses
overlapping redundant sensors

00:14:49.308 --> 00:14:51.628
to build a detailed
picture of its environment,

00:14:51.628 --> 00:14:55.088
including 13 cameras capturing
visual details like lane

00:14:55.088 --> 00:14:57.168
markings and traffic light colors,

00:14:57.168 --> 00:14:59.128
six radar sensors measuring distance

00:14:59.128 --> 00:15:01.248
and velocity, which are
helpful in conditions

00:15:01.248 --> 00:15:03.248
where cameras are less reliable,

00:15:03.248 --> 00:15:06.128
and four LiDAR sensors,
short for light detection

00:15:06.128 --> 00:15:08.608
and ranging, which send out laser pulses

00:15:08.608 --> 00:15:10.348
and measure how quickly they return,

00:15:10.348 --> 00:15:12.448
giving the car a 3D picture of shapes

00:15:12.448 --> 00:15:14.008
and distances around it.

00:15:14.008 --> 00:15:16.308
All of that data combined with microphones

00:15:16.308 --> 00:15:18.568
and high definition maps is processed

00:15:18.568 --> 00:15:20.608
by the car's onboard computer,

00:15:20.608 --> 00:15:22.788
which analyzes its surroundings, predicts

00:15:22.788 --> 00:15:25.328
what might happen next,
and decides how to act.

00:15:26.508 --> 00:15:28.928
Tesla, one of Waymo's biggest competitors,

00:15:28.928 --> 00:15:30.488
relies primarily on cameras

00:15:30.488 --> 00:15:34.568
and AI trained on millions of
miles of real-world driving,

00:15:34.568 --> 00:15:36.768
and even though its track
record is far from perfect,

00:15:38.508 --> 00:15:39.928
Elon Musk has long argued

00:15:39.928 --> 00:15:43.028
that LiDAR is costly and unnecessary.

00:15:43.028 --> 00:15:45.008
He had this to say on an earnings call.

00:15:45.008 --> 00:15:47.708
The issue with Waymo's
cars is it costs Way mo money.

00:15:49.908 --> 00:15:52.128
Trolling aside, does he have a point?

00:15:52.128 --> 00:15:54.828
Is cost still one of your
biggest barriers to scale?

00:15:54.828 --> 00:15:58.308
With every generation of
driver, we're driving down costs.

00:15:58.308 --> 00:16:00.388
There's a number of ways
that we're able to do that,

00:16:00.388 --> 00:16:04.168
but our entire hardware and
software tech stack is in-house,

00:16:04.168 --> 00:16:06.028
and that's part of how
we're able to do it.

00:16:06.028 --> 00:16:08.208
We just needed to do it at the right point

00:16:08.208 --> 00:16:10.568
with the assurance that
driving down costs wasn't going

00:16:10.568 --> 00:16:12.629
to undermine what we could achieve from a

00:16:12.629 --> 00:16:13.789
safety perspective.

00:16:16.229 --> 00:16:18.729
But the on again, off-again
relationship between Waymo

00:16:18.729 --> 00:16:21.149
and Uber is more difficult to classify.

00:16:22.889 --> 00:16:27.669
First as investor and
startup, later as rivals,

00:16:30.749 --> 00:16:32.189
and now partners in Austin

00:16:32.189 --> 00:16:34.869
and Atlanta, though their
original pilot in Phoenix

00:16:34.869 --> 00:16:35.889
ended earlier this year.

00:16:37.869 --> 00:16:40.229
How would you describe
the relationship now?

00:16:40.229 --> 00:16:43.609
Are you competitors? Are you
partners? Are you frenemies?

00:16:43.609 --> 00:16:46.189
Yeah, we're definitely partners in

00:16:46.189 --> 00:16:47.469
markets where we don't partner.

00:16:47.469 --> 00:16:49.009
We definitely compete, and I think

00:16:49.009 --> 00:16:52.089
that's been a really important
part of the relationship.

00:16:52.089 --> 00:16:54.529
In Austin and Atlanta,
we are still partners,

00:16:54.529 --> 00:16:57.009
and at the same time, we have decided

00:16:57.009 --> 00:16:59.689
that we will enter those
markets at the end of next year.

00:17:00.569 --> 00:17:03.689
But Uber, once a major
disruptor of taxi jobs,

00:17:03.689 --> 00:17:05.309
has found itself on the same side

00:17:05.309 --> 00:17:08.229
as labor unions protesting
Waymo's expansion.

00:17:08.229 --> 00:17:09.709
Whose streets? Whose jobs?

00:17:12.209 --> 00:17:15.449
Uber wants regulators to
require human drivers to be part

00:17:15.449 --> 00:17:17.489
of the ride-hailing network.

00:17:17.489 --> 00:17:19.829
Do you see that as legitimate regulation

00:17:19.829 --> 00:17:22.709
or is that a way for
Uber to defend its turf?

00:17:22.709 --> 00:17:25.509
I don't see that as a
legitimate regulation.

00:17:25.509 --> 00:17:28.349
I don't think it's relevant to autonomy.

00:17:28.349 --> 00:17:32.769
There are a lot of human jobs
required in the AV ecosystem,

00:17:32.769 --> 00:17:34.809
and we should all be championing those,

00:17:34.809 --> 00:17:36.669
but I don't think that autonomy

00:17:36.669 --> 00:17:39.969
and manned job legislation
go hand in hand.

00:17:39.969 --> 00:17:42.929
Is Waymo building a
future without drivers?

00:17:42.929 --> 00:17:45.049
Waymo is building a business

00:17:45.049 --> 00:17:47.829
where the Waymo driver is the
only driver that you need.

00:17:49.009 --> 00:17:52.749
When we show up in new
markets, we need partners

00:17:52.749 --> 00:17:57.169
who have drivers, and those
drivers help us build maps,

00:17:57.169 --> 00:17:58.909
they help us collect data.

00:17:58.909 --> 00:18:02.430
We also need people who
will work on the fleet.

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:05.810
Will some of those be people
who once drove? Maybe.

00:18:05.810 --> 00:18:06.850
And that's what we're seeing right

00:18:06.850 --> 00:18:08.390
now with some of our partners.

00:18:08.390 --> 00:18:09.650
Uber just had a big round of layoffs.

00:18:09.650 --> 00:18:12.190
Is the ride-hailing business model doomed?

00:18:12.190 --> 00:18:17.170
Earlier this year, we
surpassed 500,000 trips per week,

00:18:17.170 --> 00:18:19.050
and there's still Uber and Lyft

00:18:19.050 --> 00:18:22.650
and taxi cabs in the same
lines we're in at the airport

00:18:22.650 --> 00:18:24.210
and transporting people around.

00:18:27.030 --> 00:18:28.930
But there's another reason Waymo's cars

00:18:28.930 --> 00:18:30.450
are causing concern.

00:18:30.450 --> 00:18:32.510
As fleets of vehicles
outfitted with cameras

00:18:32.510 --> 00:18:35.570
and microphones continuously
record the streets around them,

00:18:36.870 --> 00:18:40.510
and that footage can become
evidence for law enforcement.

00:18:40.510 --> 00:18:42.730
There's definitely times
where you'll see like seven

00:18:42.730 --> 00:18:44.730
or eight of them line
up at one intersection

00:18:44.730 --> 00:18:46.790
and it gets a little like, "Oh my gosh."

00:18:46.790 --> 00:18:49.670
I see them everywhere.
They're all over the place.

00:18:49.670 --> 00:18:50.670
On every street, pretty much.

00:18:52.730 --> 00:18:55.410
There's this concern
about Waymo contributing

00:18:55.410 --> 00:18:56.850
to this surveillance culture.

00:18:56.850 --> 00:19:00.290
As it relates to law
enforcement requests for data,

00:19:00.290 --> 00:19:02.970
we down scope it as much as feasible

00:19:02.970 --> 00:19:05.690
and we only do it with lawful process.

00:19:05.690 --> 00:19:07.530
But you do share it
when they ask for it.

00:19:07.530 --> 00:19:09.190
Yeah. If it's the law

00:19:09.190 --> 00:19:10.830
and if it's within scope, we need

00:19:10.830 --> 00:19:13.610
to make sure the request
isn't unduly broad.

00:19:13.610 --> 00:19:15.070
Would ICE count as someone

00:19:15.070 --> 00:19:17.510
that you would share footage with?

00:19:17.510 --> 00:19:20.170
Yeah, I am not aware of us receiving any

00:19:20.170 --> 00:19:21.230
of those requests.

00:19:24.910 --> 00:19:28.350
I do wonder, is the Waymo
going to tell the police that I

00:19:28.350 --> 00:19:30.790
turned right on red when
I wasn't supposed to?

00:19:30.790 --> 00:19:32.010
I shouldn't be turning right on red,

00:19:32.010 --> 00:19:35.310
but I think there's a concern
that Waymo is now going

00:19:35.310 --> 00:19:37.010
to be part of this big brother

00:19:37.010 --> 00:19:38.630
that's tracking me all the time.

00:19:38.630 --> 00:19:41.390
That would require us
focusing on that, to have

00:19:41.390 --> 00:19:45.110
that capability to run the
tape back and upload it.

00:19:45.110 --> 00:19:47.611
That's just not something
that we're focused on.

00:19:47.611 --> 00:19:49.370
It's not the business that we're in.

00:19:54.320 --> 00:19:55.520
We made it.
Oh

00:19:55.520 --> 00:19:57.331
My gosh.

00:19:57.331 --> 00:19:58.651
And it's a beautiful day.

00:19:58.651 --> 00:19:59.911
Oh, it's so beautiful.

00:20:01.411 --> 00:20:02.591
I hear the beach is kind of like your

00:20:02.591 --> 00:20:04.471
thing. You come to the beach -

00:20:04.471 --> 00:20:05.471
Yeah, I love it.

00:20:05.471 --> 00:20:09.931
To decompress.
Yeah. Just be still. Stare at the ocean.

00:20:10.911 --> 00:20:13.291
During the pandemic,

00:20:13.291 --> 00:20:15.991
there's a beach only like
20 minutes from my house,

00:20:15.991 --> 00:20:19.531
and it just really served
as this place to be able

00:20:19.531 --> 00:20:22.491
to take a break from
the screen and the work.

00:20:22.491 --> 00:20:25.951
And ever since we came back,
it's just the place that I go,

00:20:25.951 --> 00:20:27.211
and time just stops.

00:20:29.831 --> 00:20:33.551
You are a female African American CEO

00:20:33.551 --> 00:20:35.531
of a big tech company.

00:20:35.531 --> 00:20:37.731
You've been asked about that many times.

00:20:37.731 --> 00:20:40.151
Are we going to get to a point ever where

00:20:40.151 --> 00:20:41.971
that is not remarkable?

00:20:41.971 --> 00:20:46.871
I hope so. I think now's the opportunity

00:20:46.871 --> 00:20:49.731
with hiring every choice you make,

00:20:49.731 --> 00:20:51.411
every person who comes along.

00:20:51.411 --> 00:20:54.571
You have the chance to
shape future leaders,

00:20:54.571 --> 00:20:58.071
and certainly I think there's plenty of

00:20:58.071 --> 00:20:59.831
female leaders out there

00:20:59.831 --> 00:21:01.351
and black leaders out there

00:21:01.351 --> 00:21:03.271
to run the companies of tomorrow.

00:21:03.271 --> 00:21:06.171
And so I'd love for it to be less

00:21:06.171 --> 00:21:08.171
of a question about being the only

00:21:09.191 --> 00:21:12.791
and just simply a question
about what experiences got me

00:21:13.651 --> 00:21:17.271
here, not in spite of that,
but in part because of that.

00:21:18.611 --> 00:21:21.171
And what are those
experiences that got you here?

00:21:21.171 --> 00:21:25.531
One of the biggest experiences
is being my mother's child.

00:21:25.531 --> 00:21:30.051
She's just someone who instilled
such fearlessness into me

00:21:30.051 --> 00:21:33.271
and just kind of an attitude of abundance.

00:21:33.271 --> 00:21:37.011
There's so much opportunity
around and why can't it be me?

00:21:38.811 --> 00:21:40.691
When you think about the future

00:21:40.691 --> 00:21:42.931
and how the transportation
industry will change,

00:21:42.931 --> 00:21:45.252
how cities will change,
how society will change,

00:21:45.252 --> 00:21:46.492
how does the world look different

00:21:46.492 --> 00:21:50.412
and what is Waymo's road ahead?

00:21:50.412 --> 00:21:53.512
I really think this
generalizable Waymo driver

00:21:53.512 --> 00:21:56.592
being deployed across
multiple business lines

00:21:56.592 --> 00:22:00.232
over the next five, 10, 20 years,

00:22:00.232 --> 00:22:03.192
this will just become
the way we think about

00:22:03.192 --> 00:22:07.412
and what future consumers
expect, which is they believe

00:22:07.412 --> 00:22:09.112
that getting safely
from point A to point B

00:22:10.172 --> 00:22:12.972
is what they deserve.

00:22:12.972 --> 00:22:14.132
It's the expectation

00:22:14.132 --> 00:22:17.992
and we just haven't had a
solution to that problem.

00:22:17.992 --> 00:22:19.972
So you think Waymo will be in every car?

00:22:19.972 --> 00:22:22.612
I think that Waymo will
be available to people

00:22:22.612 --> 00:22:24.712
who want that option.

00:22:24.712 --> 00:22:26.352
A lot of people like driving

00:22:26.352 --> 00:22:29.892
and I want those people to drive
when they can pay attention

00:22:29.892 --> 00:22:31.232
and when it's enjoyable.

00:22:31.232 --> 00:22:33.272
What do you drive now?
I drive a fast car.

00:22:34.492 --> 00:22:36.952
Okay. Do you think
this is your last car?

00:22:36.952 --> 00:22:40.772
No, because I am that
person who will have Waymo

00:22:40.772 --> 00:22:43.512
and I am that person who will have a car

00:22:43.512 --> 00:22:45.252
with my music blasting.

00:22:45.252 --> 00:22:46.872
And I will continue to do that

00:22:46.872 --> 00:22:50.712
because I also think it's
important to be clear

00:22:50.712 --> 00:22:53.672
that I think there will
continue to be a choice

00:22:53.672 --> 00:22:56.252
and Waymo's not going to be

00:22:56.252 --> 00:22:58.752
anytime soon in my car, and that's okay.

00:22:59.692 --> 00:23:01.532
There will be many, many car manufacturers

00:23:01.532 --> 00:23:03.352
around the world that'll
continue to make cars

00:23:03.352 --> 00:23:04.492
for people who want to drive them.

00:23:39.853 --> 00:23:42.073
It does feel like self-driving
cars have been right

00:23:42.073 --> 00:23:45.453
around the corner every
year for the last 20 years,

00:23:45.453 --> 00:23:49.033
but here we are still driving
in the old-fashioned way.

